@startuml
title WireClaim — how a Charge and a Limit are decided

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam rectangleBorderThickness 1

rectangle "**RunManager.snapshot()** — recomputed on every event" as Snap {

  rectangle "1. standard  (source: standard)\n a = 100 x qty      b = 75 x qty\n//always present — the floor//" as L1 #F4F4F4
  rectangle "2. fast_path_llm\n one LLM call, 20 s\n//overlays 1 where it has an index//" as L2 #EAF3FB
  rectangle "3. strategy  (router winner)\n strategy2 (prio 2)  >  strategy1 (prio 1)\n//overlays 1-2//" as L3 #E7F5EC
  rectangle "4. fraud overlay\n if index in fraud_indices:  **b := 0**\n//a is never touched//" as L4 #FDECEC

  L1 -down-> L2
  L2 -down-> L3
  L3 -down-> L4
}

rectangle "Submitted\n(index, charge_price, acceptance_limit)" as Out #FFF6DA
L4 -down-> Out

note right of L3
  **Deterministic pricing** (identical maths
  in fast_path.py and strategy1):

    median = (low + high) / 2
    fallback = 150 x qty
    **a = max(fallback, 0.7 x median)**      (R5b)
    **b = Q(1/3) of the spike-and-slab**     (R4, R6)
        clamped to [0, median]

  confirmed_uncovered  =>  b = 0, a kept
  //an uncovered item is still charged//    (R6c)
end note

note bottom of L4
  Only a **verbatim policy quote** may
  produce a fraud verdict — a suspicious
  Damage Description may not (Case 7).
end note
@enduml
